// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: a map whose declared value is a union ARM, in a program that also
// declares a standalone struct of that arm's EXACT layout. The map's own binding is
// inferred, so its slot is keyed on `tyToEmitName`'s RENDER of the value (`{r:i32}`), the
// render resolves through the struct table to `Dot`, and the annotated `thru` parameter's
// slot is keyed on the arm. Two mv slots over two vals element heaps —
// `uVarHeap[Circle]` and `sHeapIdx[Dot]` — and no seam merges them.
//
// NINE LINES, no import, no generic, no lambda, no `??`. `vl check` exits 0 with NO
// diagnostics at all and is the ONLY command that misses it; `vl check --codegen` exits 1,
// `vl check --codegen --no-validate` exits 0, `vl build` writes the `.wasm` and exits 1,
// `vl run` exits 1:
//
//   the emitted module is not valid wasm … type mismatch:
//   expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)
//
// (No byte offset quoted on purpose: it moves with the seed. NOTE the message has no
//  `ref null` in it — unlike its three predecessors this one does not even look like a
//  nullability defect, and it is not one. The two `$type`s are the arm's variant-table heap
//  and the twin's struct-table heap, which `DECISIONS.md` keeps in two namespaces on
//  purpose: "the variant/struct-TABLE seam stays nominal".)
//
// WHY IT IS NOT THE ROW THAT SHIPS WITH IT. D123's close merged the two mv slots wherever
// they had interned the SAME vals ref-list slot — which is every `arm` and `armdiff` cell
// of the D88/D100 grid, 56 of its 100 survivors. Here they have NOT: with `Dot` declared,
// `structIndexByValName("{r:i32}")` finds it before anything can ask whether an arm of that
// layout exists, so the two slots hold two genuinely different heaps and merging them would
// be wrong. Probed at the mint on the two-level sibling of this program:
//
//   slot=0 name=Circle   kind=1 canon=1 rl=0 rlwrap=9   (rl=0 nm=Circle heap=1)
//   slot=1 name={r:i32}  kind=1 canon=1 rl=1 rlwrap=11  (rl=1 nm=Dot    heap=0)
//
// THE DECIDING INFORMATION EXISTS AND IS NOT AT THE MINT. `thru`'s annotation says the
// value is `Circle`; the literal's own recorded arena type is a FRESH anonymous `TyObj`
// (`variantRowOfTy` = -1, `structIndexOfTy` = -1), so nothing the mv layer is handed can
// pick between `Circle` and `Dot`. This is D39's channel problem — "an anonymous `{ r: n }`
// has two nominal claimants and only the CONTEXT separates them" — at the map-VALUE
// position, and the two shapes that already carry a context to an un-annotated binding
// (`synthRetPinAnn`, `synthEmptyListAnn`) do not reach it. A loud floor at the mint was
// measured on the neighbouring row (D93's header: 866/148/10 against 894/112/18) and buys
// 8 fewer silent cells for 28 fewer working programs, so it is not the answer either.
//
// SIX CONTROLS, all run verbatim against the tree this file ships with:
//   delete `type Dot`                       -> LOUD emit reject (unsupported map value type)
//   `type Dot = { q: i32 }` (a non-twin)    -> LOUD emit reject (same message)
//   delete `type Shape = Circle | Sq`       -> RUNS
//   annotate `c: {[string]: Circle}`        -> RUNS
//   wrap the two statements in a function   -> RUNS  (but see the next paragraph)
//   the same program at two nesting levels  -> check-clean invalid wasm, `ref null` message
//
// THE PASSING CONTROL WAS INTERROGATED, NOT COUNTED. "Wrap it in a function and it runs"
// does NOT mean the slot split is absent there. Probed, the two programs intern the SAME
// two mv slots over the SAME two ref-list rows (`rl=0 nm=Circle heap=1 wrap=9`,
// `rl=1 nm=Dot heap=0 wrap=11`) and emit BYTE-IDENTICAL type sections — both carry the
// `Circle`-vals map struct and the `Dot`-vals one. What differs is which of them the
// BINDING's `Map()` constructor resolves: `struct.new 15` at function scope against
// `struct.new 16` at module scope. So this row is a binding-RESOLUTION problem as much as a
// mint one, and that is the cheapest next probe. WHERE THE MAP IS BOUND is the axis neither
// the D88/D100 nor the D112 grid varies — both build it as a function local at every cell —
// and `scripts/silent-sweep/d139/` is the 36-cell grid that does: at
// `armtwin x mapval x route=none` the LOCAL runs while the GLOBAL and the CALL-RESULT
// bindings are silent, on master and on the branch alike.
//
// PRE-EXISTING on `89f88840`, same message — not a regression from the change this file
// ships with; the branch's module for it is byte-identical to master's. Filed as
// `silent-class-inventory.md` D139; it is 44 of the 100 D88/D100-grid survivors and 12 of
// the 61 D112-grid survivors, and after D123/D124 it is the WHOLE of both residues.
//
// IT IS THE SPECIMEN. This file is kept byte-for-byte identical to
// `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts`'s `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` below its header, which needs a
// program that type-checks clean and whose module the engine refuses.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, graduate a `@run` row, and swap `INVALID_MODULE_SRC` for another live member
// of the class. READ THE NOTE IN `tests/vl_check_codegen_test.ts` BEFORE DOING THAT —
// including what it says about where to look before concluding the class is empty, and the
// sixth source it carries: re-grade an EARLIER change's grid against today's master.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }
function thru(x: {[string]: Circle}) { return x }
const c = Map()
c["o"] = { r: 7 }
thru(c)
print(7)
